---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import RecordMap from "../components/RecordMap.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import ArchiveShowcase from "../components/ArchiveShowcase.astro";
import idx from "../../public/index.json";
import regions from "../../public/regions.json";
import provs from "../../public/providers.json";

const places = idx.places.length;
const volumes = idx.places.reduce((a, p) => a + (p.c || 0), 0);
const countries = new Set(idx.places.map(p => p.k).filter(Boolean)).size;
const archives = provs.providers.filter(p => p.at);
const archiveCountries = new Set(archives.map(p => p.at.place.split(", ").pop())).size;
---
<Base
  bare
  title="Record Atlas"
  dek="Every place your people came from, and who holds the paper.">

  {/* TIGHTER THAN THE PROSE PAGES, BECAUSE THIS ONE IS A TOOL. The shared
      .pagehead gives every page 56px of air above the title and holds the
      standfirst to a 76ch measure — right for something you read, wrong for
      something you use, where it pushed the map below the fold and left a
      third of a wide screen blank. .ra-lede takes the air back and lets the
      line run the full width. The wording is two sentences now; the old five
      spent three of them clearing their throat before the point. */}
  <div class="pagehead ra-lede">
    <div class="lab">{places} places · {volumes} record volumes · {archives.length} archives in {archiveCountries} countries · {regions.regions.length} record regions</div>
    <h1>Where the records are</h1>
    <p class="dek">
      Click the place your people came from: every collection that covers it,
      coloured by <em>what it actually costs to look</em>. Set a year and it also names
      whose empire held that ground — and therefore whose archive to write to.
    </p>
  </div>

  <RecordMap
    indexUrl={u("/index.json")}
    detailBase={u("/p/")}
    regionsUrl={u("/regions.json")}
    providersUrl={u("/providers.json")}
    gazBase={u("/g/")}
    surnameBase={u("/s/")}
    wikidataUrl={u("/archives-wikidata.json")}
    osmUrl={u("/archives-osm.json")}
    featureBase={u("/")}
    settlementBase={u("/rs/")}
    districtBase={u("/a/")}
    countryBase={u("/c/")}
    townlandBase={u("/t/")}
    comuneBase={u("/it/")}
    estoniaBase={u("/ee/")}
    scotlandBase={u("/sc/")}
    worldArchivesUrl={u("/world-archives.json")}
    countryPointsUrl={u("/country-points.json")}
    base={u("/")} />

  <div class="one" style="margin-top:34px">
    <div class="lab">Why a map, and not another list</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><b>Three things stand between a researcher and a record, and all three are geographic.</b></p>
      <p><b>The name changed.</b> Gallignana is Gračišće. Fiume is Rijeka. Pola is Pula.
      You cannot search for a place whose name you do not know, so the find box here matches
      every name a place has ever had — type <em>Gallignana</em> and the map goes to Gračišće.
      This works <b>worldwide</b>, well outside the shelf: <em>Breslau</em> answers Wrocław,
      <em>Czernowitz</em> answers Chernivtsi, <em>Memel</em> answers Klaipėda.</p>
      <p><b>The jurisdiction changed.</b> An 1863 baptism from a Croatian village sits in a
      Croatian archive. A 1930 one, from the same village, is filed under <em>Italy</em> —
      because between the wars that village was Italian. Searching the Croatian collection
      alone finds nothing, and nothing is not the same as not existing. Set the year and this
      map tells you which it is.</p>
      <p><b>"Online" is four different things.</b> Free images, images behind a login, an index
      with no images at all, and a catalogue card for paper nobody has photographed. Most
      directories flatten these into a link. Here they are the colour of the dot.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="one" style="margin-top:26px;border-left-color:var(--rule-strong)">
    <div class="lab">What this map does not know yet</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>It is honest about being early, and the three layers are at three different stages.
      <b>The archives layer is worldwide</b> — {archives.length} institutions across
      {archiveCountries} countries, each saying what it holds and what it costs to look.
      <b>The shelf is deep in one corner</b>: {places} places and {volumes} volumes across
      Croatia, Istria and Carnia, and thin to the point of empty everywhere else.
      <b>The jurisdiction layer covers {regions.regions.length} pieces of ground</b>, not the
      planet — Istria and the Kvarner, Carnia, Dalmatia, Campania, eastern Sicily, Calabria,
      Prussian Posen, the eastern Cape, England, Queensland and Santa Fe. A place outside them
      says so in its own panel rather than guessing, because a confident wrong answer about
      which archive to write to costs somebody a month.</p>
      <p><b>A grey dot means nobody has surveyed it. It does not mean nothing is there.</b>
      That distinction is the difference between a map and a claim, and it is worth more
      than coverage.</p>
      <p><a href={u("/add/")}>Add a source, or correct one →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <ArchiveShowcase u={u} />
</Base>
